# Waves, guess factors and a KD-tree

Topic 08, talk 1

After this talk you can:

- draw a bullet as a wave and say what a guess factor measures
- explain why a KD-tree beats a scan of every past wave
- read Hadur's `Wave`, `KdTree` and `MainGun`

---

## A bullet is a circle

When a robot fires, the bullet leaves from one point at a fixed speed.

- Speed is `20 - 3 * power` pixels per tick (power 3 is 11 px/tick).
- After `t` ticks the bullet is somewhere on a circle of radius `speed * t`.
- You do not know where on the circle. That is the whole problem.

Hadur keeps this in one class, `hadur2.core.model.Wave`.

```java
public double distanceTraveled(long currentTime) {
    return (currentTime - fireTime) * bulletSpeed;
}
```

---

## The target cannot be anywhere

The target can only move so far sideways before the bullet arrives.

At top speed (8 px/tick) moving square to the line of fire, the farthest it gets off the line is `asin(8 / bulletSpeed)`.

```java
this.bulletSpeed = 20.0 - 3.0 * power;
this.maxEscapeAngle = Math.asin(8.0 / bulletSpeed);
```

(`Wave.java`, in `setBulletPower`.)

A power 3 bullet gives about 0.81 radians. A power 1 bullet gives about 0.49.

---

## The guess factor

Measure the angle from the head-on line, then divide by the escape angle.

- `0` means the target stayed on the head-on line.
- `+1` means it went the full escape angle the way it was already orbiting.
- `-1` means the full escape angle the other way.

```java
public double guessAngle(double bearingToTarget) {
    return orbitDirection
        * Angles.normalRelativeAngle(bearingToTarget - absBearing);
}
public double guessFactor(double bearingToTarget) {
    return guessAngle(bearingToTarget) / maxEscapeAngle;
}
```

---

## Why scale and mirror?

Two reasons, both in the Javadoc of `Wave`.

- Scaling by the escape angle lets a shot at power 1 teach a shot at power 3.
- Mirroring by the orbit direction lets a clockwise sample teach a counter-clockwise one.

So the gun gets more use out of every sample it has.

The reverse mapping is one line:

```java
public double firingAngle(double guessFactor) {
    return absBearing + guessFactor * orbitDirection * maxEscapeAngle;
}
```

---

## Learning from a wave

The loop for a gun is short.

1. Fire (or imagine firing) and store a wave with a snapshot of the situation.
2. When the wave reaches the target, work out where the target really was.
3. Save that outcome next to the snapshot.
4. Next time, find past waves with a similar snapshot and aim where their outcomes cluster.

Step 4 is a nearest-neighbour search. Everything else is bookkeeping.

---

## Hadur stores displacement, not just a factor

Hadur's `MainGun` keeps where the target went as a displacement vector and replays it from the new position. A projected point outside the field is dropped.

```java
Point2D.Double projected = w.projectLocationBlind(
    myNextLocation, dispVector, currentTime);
if (battleField.rectangle.contains(projected)) {
    firingAngles[i] = DiaUtils.absoluteBearing(
        myNextLocation, projected);
    valid[i] = true;
}
```

A guess factor cannot say "that point is in a wall". A replayed path can. The anti-surfer gun and the surf's danger views use guess factors.

---

## k nearest neighbours

A situation is a short list of numbers: distance, speed, lateral speed, wall distances. The "most like this one" waves are the k closest points.

Brute force compares the query with every stored wave. That is O(n) per query, and Hadur asks every tick.

A KD-tree splits the points by space so most of them are never looked at.

- Leaves hold a bucket of up to 24 points.
- A full leaf splits at the midpoint of its widest axis.
- Every node keeps a bounding box of what is below it.

---

## The search skips whole subtrees

Go down the side of each split the query is on. On the way back up, visit the other side only if its bounding box could hold something nearer than the k-th best so far.

```java
if (cursor.status == Status.ALLVISITED
        && (nextCursor.locationCount == 0
        || (!nextCursor.singularity
        && root.pointRegionDist(location,
            nextCursor.minLimit, nextCursor.maxLimit) > range))) {
    continue;   // prune this subtree
}
```

The k best so far live in a max-heap, so the worst of them is always at index 0 (`range = resultHeap.getMaxDist()`).

---

## The heap is plain arrays

Hadur's `ResultHeap` is two parallel arrays, kept as a max-heap on distance.

```java
void addValue(double dist, Object value) {
    if (this.values < this.size) {
        this.data[this.values] = value;
        this.distance[this.values] = dist;
        upHeapify(this.values);
        this.values++;
    } else if (dist < this.distance[0]) {
        this.data[0] = value;
        this.distance[0] = dist;
        downHeapify(0);
    }
}
```

In the lab you will use `PriorityQueue` with a reversed `Comparator` for the same job. Same idea, less code, more boxing.

---

## Bounded, deterministic, tested

- A size limit makes the tree a FIFO: the oldest point goes first. That keeps every view bounded (RES-2).
- Results depend only on the points and their order, so replays stay exact (CORE-2).
- Distances are squared and weighted: `sum(((a[i] - b[i]) * w[i])^2)`, no square root.

In the lab you prove your tree right the way Hadur proves its shadows right: a jqwik property that compares it with a brute-force search on random points.

Source: `hadur-core/.../knn/KdTree.java`, `.../model/Wave.java`, `.../gun/MainGun.java`.

